WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.063
Unit 2 is the standard's callout:
machines run the steps, people decide.

2
00:00:05.063 --> 00:00:10.165
Every step a machine or a script can run,
it runs: the request's checks, the plan,

3
00:00:10.165 --> 00:00:14.444
the Active Directory writes, the
re-read that closes the ticket,

4
00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:18.724
the monitoring, the certification
campaigns and the evidence.

5
00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:21.847
People decide, approve in
SailPoint, accept risk,

6
00:00:21.847 --> 00:00:26.375
and run the few steps that need Domain
Admins or that delete something.

7
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.219
This lines up with the
federal requirements better

8
00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:32.063
than the same work done by
hand in a console or portal.

9
00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:39.488
NIST SP 800-53 has enhancements for
automated account management, disabling

10
00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:44.387
accounts and automated audit actions: A
C two, enhancements one, three and four.

11
00:00:44.387 --> 00:00:50.263
FedRAMP's K S I dash I A M dash A A
M is Automating Account Management.

12
00:00:50.263 --> 00:00:53.781
And CISA's Zero Trust Maturity
Model makes automation

13
00:00:53.781 --> 00:00:56.763
and orchestration a
cross-cutting capability.

14
00:00:56.763 --> 00:00:59.863
Automation has limits, and
the standard keeps them.

15
00:00:59.863 --> 00:01:02.291
Separation of duties keeps the approver,

16
00:01:02.291 --> 00:01:06.433
the pipeline's owner and the person
who deletes apart, the authorizing

17
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:10.862
official accepts the pipeline, and the
pipeline's own identity is governed.

18
00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:14.540
Automation supports a requirement;
the assessor decides.
